   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.2. Increased capacity of Member States to strengthen policies and strategies to promote and ensure equal opportunities, participation and treatment between women and men, including equal remuneration for work of equal value</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Four entities have successfully conducted a Participatory Gender Audit (PGA) namely The National Federation of Trade Unions of Workers and Employees of Lebanon (FENASOL), the General Confederation of Lebanese Workers (CGTL), the association of Lebanese Industrialists (ALI), and the Lebanese League for Women in Business (LLWB). This important undertaking was achieved by workers&apos; organizations, and employers&apos; organizations, demonstrating their commitment to fostering gender equality within the workplace.

The PGA process culminated in the identification of key recommendations aimed at advancing gender equality. These recommendations were designed to create a foundation for equal opportunities and fair treatment of both men and women within these organizations. Subsequently, each of the four entities developed comprehensive action plans. These plans, meticulously tailored to the unique needs of each organization, serve as essential tools for tracking progress and establishing well-informed roadmaps towards achieving meaningful gender parity in the realm of work.

The association of Lebanese industrialists established a Women in Management Fund that funds capacity building initiatives based on the results of assessments and gender audits, for women to return and progress in work. 20% of the 75 Women who received training got the opportunity of playing new and/or more important roles in their company through acquiring new responsibilities, 25% cascaded the training to their colleagues in the company and more than 40% considered that some of the trainings received has helped them improve their communication skills and surpass conflicts. Furthermore, 20% shared that the skills acquired through leadership and management trainings have helped them become more effective leaders and managers, enabling them to better motivate and lead their teams, ultimately increasing productivity. Under the Women in Management Initiative, the ILO RBSA project, has played a significant role in facilitating the completion of a participatory Gender Audit for four key entities: FENASOL, CGTL, ALI and LLWB. Through this collaboration, ILO has contributed expertise and guidance to enhance gender equality by advocating for equitable opportunities and treatment for both men and women. The participatory gender audit process led to the formulation of actionable recommendations, laying the foundation for progress. ILO&apos;s involvement has extended to the development of comprehensive action plans for each entity, enabling them to systematically gauge their advancements and create well-informed pathways towards achieving lasting gender equality.


ILO designed the Women in Management Fund and contributed to the finances from which training was offered, through the MALKIA program at ITCILO, ILO contributed to  delivering leadership training to 15 women. Employers contributed in kind through the use of their venues and other support. The ILO funded the training needs assessments that preceded the training delivery.</narrative>
   </description>
